\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The paired Yukawa determinant over a ring}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{For the \vocabk{Yukawa map}{fdip-011M}, suppose the fermion
fibre is finite free, so the total cochain module #{\mathcal V}
is finite free of rank #{N}. Use the universal odd vector #{\psi},
odd covector #{\bar\psi}, and \vocabk{ring paired coefficient}{fdip-00TJ}.
Set #{\theta_{K}=-\bar\psi(K\psi)} for #{K=K_{U,\phi}}, with
the barred coefficient multiplying first. The onsite contribution
to this quadratic element is #{-\bar\psi(Y_\phi\psi)}. Then
##{\mathcal I_{\mathcal V}\extexp(\theta_K)=\det K.}
Both the determinant and the joint weight #{\Xi(U,\phi)\det K}
are invariant under vertex gauges. For #{A=\extD_U+Y_\phi}
one has the finite polynomial identity
##{\det(m\bbI+A)=
 \sum_{j=0}^N m^{N-j}\trace(\Lambda^jA).}
These identities include singular kernels over every commutative
unital coefficient ring.}

\proof{
\p{The contraction is linear in the endomorphism, so its three
terms are the mass, cochain and onsite contributions with the
same barred-first sign. Apply \ref{fdip-00TL} with its canonical
paired top orientation to obtain the determinant, with no division
by factorials. Under a vertex gauge the two odd arguments
transform as a vector and a dual covector. The induced change
on the exterior algebra is that of \ref{fdip-00TW}; it preserves
the top coefficient and transports the kernel by conjugation
as in \ref{fdip-011N}. Conjugation preserves the determinant,
and the scalar and connection weight is invariant by
\ref{fdip-010C}. Finally apply \ref{fdip-00TZ} to #{-A}.
Since #{\Lambda^j(-A)=(-1)^j\Lambda^j A}, the two signs cancel.
The zeroth exterior trace is one.}
}

\p{Conjugation invariance of the determinant is formalized by
\leanref{LinearMap.det_conj}.}
